\name{InferencePropQuantileRegr}
\alias{InferencePropQuantileRegr}
\title{Quantile Regression Inference for Proportion Responses}
\description{
Fits a quantile regression for proportion responses (constrained to (0, 1)) using
the treatment indicator and, optionally, all recorded covariates as predictors.
Inference is performed on the \strong{logit (log-odds) scale}: responses
\eqn{y \in (0,1)} are transformed via \eqn{\text{logit}(y) = \log(y/(1-y))} before
quantile regression, so the estimated treatment effect is a \strong{log-odds-ratio
shift} at quantile \code{tau}; by default \code{tau = 0.5}, so this is a median
log-odds-ratio shift.

Fitting is via \code{\link[quantreg]{rq}} (method \code{"br"}, the Barrodale-Roberts
simplex algorithm, for the point estimate; the default Frisch-Newton-adjacent
interior-point path for the variance-computing fit) on \code{logit(y) ~ w + covariates}
with no intercept column (the design matrix already carries one). Standard errors use
\pkg{quantreg}'s Powell (1991) kernel sandwich \code{"nid"} estimator (heteroskedasticity-
and design-robust, valid under non-i.i.d. errors) when available, falling back to the
i.i.d.-errors \code{"iid"} estimator if \code{"nid"} extraction fails; inference on the
resulting standard error uses the asymptotic normal (Wald) approximation, not a
resampling-based reference distribution, for the asymptotic CI/p-value paths.
\code{compute_asymp_confidence_interval}/\code{compute_asymp_two_sided_pval} use the
fit's residual degrees of freedom \eqn{n - p} in a \eqn{t}-reference (via
\code{compute_z_or_t_ci_from_s_and_df}) rather than a plain normal reference, so the
interval/test remain slightly conservative in small samples relative to a bare Wald z.

This class requires the \pkg{quantreg} package, which is listed under
\code{Suggests} and is not installed automatically with \pkg{EDI}.
Install \pkg{quantreg} manually before use. Only uncensored proportion responses are
supported (checked via \code{assertNoCensoring} at construction).
}
\section{Small-sample behaviour}{

How fast the size (and coverage) error of each procedure is expected to vanish as the
effective sample size \eqn{n_{eff}}{n_eff} grows, from EDI's convergence-rate registry
(\code{inference_convergence_expectation()}). \emph{Exact} procedures are valid at every
n by construction; the others are asymptotic, so a small-sample excess is expected, and
its constant is not given by an order. The table is the expectation for a balanced
complete-randomization design; it is not a measured size. Reference keys are explained
under References.

\tabular{lllllll}{\strong{Procedure} \tab \strong{Regime} \tab \strong{Error order} \tab \strong{Effective n} \tab \strong{Regularity} \tab \strong{Basis} \tab \strong{References} \cr
\code{rand} \tab finite sample \tab exact \tab subjects \tab regular \tab theory \tab [Fisher1935], [LehmannRomano2005] \cr
\code{wald} \tab asymptotic \tab \eqn{O(n_{eff}^{-2/3})}{O(n_eff^-2/3)} \tab subjects \tab nonparametric rate \tab literature \tab [HallSheather1988], [Koenker1994], [Koenker2005] \cr
\code{jackknife} \tab asymptotic \tab \eqn{O(n_{eff}^{-1})}{O(n_eff^-1)} \tab subjects \tab regular \tab assumed default \tab [ShaoTu1995] \cr
\code{bootstrap} \tab asymptotic \tab \eqn{O(n_{eff}^{-1})}{O(n_eff^-1)} \tab subjects \tab regular \tab assumed default \tab [Hall1992] \cr
\code{bayes_boot} \tab asymptotic \tab \eqn{O(n_{eff}^{-1})}{O(n_eff^-1)} \tab subjects \tab regular \tab assumed default \tab [Rubin1981], [Lo1987] \cr
\code{rand_bootstrap} \tab asymptotic \tab \eqn{O(n_{eff}^{-1})}{O(n_eff^-1)} \tab subjects \tab regular \tab assumed default \tab [Hall1992], [Kallus2018]}

The point estimate's bias is of order \eqn{O(n_{eff}^{-1})}{O(n_eff^-1)} (assumed default: [CoxSnell1968]).

Rows with basis \emph{assumed default}: the order shown is the procedure's rate in a
regular parametric model, and the cited works establish it for that case only. EDI has not
derived it for this class.

Regularity \emph{nonparametric rate}: the standard error needs a nonparametric (kernel sparsity) estimate, so the rate is slower than the parametric one.

Design-specific differences from the table:
\itemize{
\item On \code{DesignFixedBernoulli (prob_T = 0.2)}, for \code{wald}, \code{jackknife}, \code{bootstrap}, \code{bayes_boot}, \code{rand_bootstrap}: the effective n is the size of the smaller arm.
\item On \code{DesignFixedBlockedCluster}, \code{DesignFixedCluster}, for \code{wald}, \code{jackknife}, \code{bootstrap}, \code{bayes_boot}, \code{rand_bootstrap}: the effective n is the number of clusters.
}
}

\section{Blocking designs}{

On a blocking design
(\code{\link[EDI:DesignFixedBlocking]{DesignFixedBlocking}} and relatives)
the regression-type inference classes do \emph{not} add block fixed effects
(or block-by-treatment interactions) automatically: the model uses only the
covariate matrix. Whether the blocks enter the regression therefore depends
on how they were supplied: not at all with \code{m=}; as dummies when the
blocking column is a factor/character (or few-level integer) covariate; as a
single linear term when it is numeric. Randomization, bootstrap and
jackknife inference are block-aware regardless; only the model-based
(asymptotic, likelihood) path ignores the blocks.

When the proportion treated is the same in every block, the pooled
treatment estimate of a linear model is unbiased and its model-based SE is
conservative (it is inflated by the block variation it fails to remove), at a
cost in power. If the proportion treated differs across blocks (for example
\code{equal_block_sizes = FALSE} with very small blocks and an extreme
\code{prob_T}, where per-block rounding changes the allocation, or a
user-supplied \code{m}), the pooled treated-vs-control comparison is
confounded by block (Simpson's paradox) and can be biased; then include the
blocking covariate(s) explicitly as factor covariates. For non-linear models
(GLM, ordinal, survival) omitting blocks changes the estimand to a
block-marginal effect. See \code{DesignFixedBlocking}.
}

\examples{
if (requireNamespace("quantreg", quietly = TRUE)) {
\donttest{
seq_des = DesignSeqOneByOneBernoulli$new(n = 10, response_type = 'proportion')
for (i in 1:10) {
  seq_des$add_one_subject_to_experiment_and_assign(data.frame(x1 = rnorm(1)))
}
seq_des$add_all_subject_responses(runif(10))
inf = InferencePropQuantileRegr$new(seq_des)
inf$compute_estimate()
}
}
}
